<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="390b6c69">&lt;p&gt;
    A work breakdown structure (WBS) integrates the project scope and defines activities and tasks as part of the approach.
    The WBS helps define all the work that is necessary to complete the project and serves as the foundation for creating a
    project plan. It is work product oriented and hierarchical, and decomposes the overall work into smaller elements.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    WBS with a branch decomposed down through the activity level
&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_med.base/guidances/concepts/./resources/PM-600-0009.png&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;519&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The WBS addresses the execution of a project at defined, detailed levels as follows:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Level 1 is used for the project phase. 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Highest level of the project or systems life cycle
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Level 2 is used for project activities. 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Logical combination of tasks needed to build a work product.
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Level 3 is used for project tasks.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li style=&quot;LIST-STYLE-TYPE: none&quot;&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Unit of work at which resources expend effort.
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    The WBS establishes a common framework to track responsibility for each element and facilitates the tracking of the
    schedule, cost and performance metrics.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    The WBS activities, tasks and supporting details become inputs to the prospective schedule. The development of the
    schedule should follow a logical order that properly sequences work (i.e., activities and tasks) to meet project
    objectives. Factors for this process include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            What order of execution activities is necessary to reach the objective?
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            What are the required start and end dates?
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            What are the scheduling dependencies?
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Dependencies between activities are also determined during the Initiate process and are further refined as additional
    information is obtained. These dependencies are categorized as follows:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            Finish-to-start precedence (one activity must finish before another starts – example, cannot make a cake until
            after the ingredients are purchased)
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            Start-to-start precedence (two tasks can commence at the same time – example, can interview and gather
            documentation at the same time)
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            Finish-to-finish precedence (finish of one task determines the finish of another task – example, when assessor
            finishes report review the team can determine when to finalize the report)
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            Start-to-finish precedence (start of one task determines the finish of another task – example, when a vendor
            comes onboard the finish of the project can be estimated)
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    These general scheduling concerns can be addressed through additional planning of project activities.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
